    "q-111396": {
      "dom_id": "step-q111396",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find a basis of the null space of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$",
      "worked_solution": "To find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $Ax=0$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\nThe given matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. So, the equation $Ax=0$ corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+4{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}+2{x}_{4}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=2{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{2}=-4{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{3}=-2{x}_{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}$ are basic variables corresponding to the pivot columns ($1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd) and ${x}_{4}$ is a free variable corresponding to the non-pivot column. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{4} \\\\ -4{x}_{4} \\\\ -2{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{4}\\in R.$ This implies that\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate the $\\text{Null}(A).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-111396-a",
              "type": "math",
