    "q-34522": {
      "dom_id": "step-q34522",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "The matrices $A$ and $B$ are given below. Given that $A$ is row equivalent to $B$, then find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "To find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $Ax=0$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\nSince $A∼B,$ the equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the equation $Bx=0,$ which corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=0,$ where ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
